\subsection{Piece State Machine}

Each piece in a torrent download progresses through a state machine that tracks
its download status. Understanding these states and their transitions is
fundamental to correct piece management.

A piece begins in the [[Missing]] state, indicating we have not yet received
any blocks for it. Once the first block arrives, the piece transitions to
[[InProgress]]. The [[InProgress]] state carries a [[BitVec]] that tracks which
blocks have been received---this enables two important behaviors:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Resumption}: If a peer disconnects mid-piece, we can resume
  downloading from another peer without re-requesting blocks we already have.
\item \textbf{Duplicate detection}: If two peers send the same block (due to
  overlapping requests or reconnection scenarios), we can detect and ignore
  the duplicate.
\end{enumerate}

When all blocks have been received, we compute the SHA1 hash and compare it
against the expected hash from the metainfo. If the hashes match, the piece
transitions to [[Verified]]---it has been written to disk and is complete.
If the hashes do not match, the piece transitions to [[Failed]].

The [[Failed]] state is deliberately distinct from [[Missing]]. While a failed
piece will be re-downloaded, we may want to track which pieces have failed
verification (and from which peers) to detect malicious or buggy peers. A piece
in [[Failed]] state can transition back to [[InProgress]] when we retry the
download.

\begin{verbatim}
                                 +----------+
                                 |  Missing |
                                 +----+-----+
                                      |
                             first block received
                                      |
                                      v
                               +------+------+
                          +--->| InProgress  |<---+
                          |    +------+------+    |
                          |           |           |
                       retry     all blocks      retry
                          |      received         |
                          |           |           |
                          |           v           |
                          |     hash check        |
                          |      /      \         |
                          |    ok        fail     |
                          |    /            \     |
                          |   v              v    |
                       +--+---+          +---+--+ |
                       |Verified|        |Failed+-+
                       +-------+         +------+
\end{verbatim}

<<piece state>>=
use bitvec::prelude::*;

/// The download state of a single piece.
///
/// This enum models the state machine for piece downloads. Each piece starts
/// as [`Missing`](PieceState::Missing), progresses through
/// [`InProgress`](PieceState::InProgress) as blocks are received, and ends
/// in either [`Verified`](PieceState::Verified) (success) or
/// [`Failed`](PieceState::Failed) (hash mismatch).
///
/// # State Transitions
///
/// - `Missing` -> `InProgress`: First block of the piece is received
/// - `InProgress` -> `Verified`: All blocks received and hash matches
/// - `InProgress` -> `Failed`: All blocks received but hash does not match
/// - `Failed` -> `InProgress`: Retry download after failure
///
/// # Design Notes
///
/// The `InProgress` variant carries a `BitVec` rather than a simple block
/// count because we need to track *which* blocks have been received, not just
/// how many. This is essential for:
///
/// 1. **Resumption**: If a connection drops, we can continue from where we
///    left off by requesting only the missing blocks.
/// 2. **Deduplication**: Multiple peers might send the same block; we can
///    detect and ignore duplicates.
/// 3. **Request scheduling**: We can request different blocks from different
///    peers to parallelize downloads.
///
/// The `Failed` state is separate from `Missing` to support tracking of
/// verification failures. This enables:
///
/// 1. **Peer reputation**: Track which peers contributed to failed pieces
/// 2. **Retry limits**: Avoid infinite retry loops on corrupted data
/// 3. **Debugging**: Log which pieces repeatedly fail verification
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub enum PieceState {
    /// The piece has not been downloaded and no blocks have been received.
    ///
    /// This is the initial state for all pieces when a download begins, or
    /// after resuming a download that had no partial data for this piece.
    Missing,

    /// The piece is being downloaded; some blocks have been received.
    ///
    /// The contained `BitVec` tracks which blocks have been received, with
    /// one bit per block. The bit at index `i` is set if block `i` has been
    /// received. The length of the `BitVec` equals the total number of blocks
    /// in this piece.
    ///
    /// # Block Indexing
    ///
    /// Blocks are indexed from 0 to `num_blocks - 1`. For a standard 256 KiB
    /// piece with 16 KiB blocks, there are 16 blocks indexed 0..16. The last
    /// piece of a torrent may have fewer blocks if the file size is not
    /// evenly divisible.
    InProgress {
        /// Bit vector tracking which blocks have been received.
        /// `blocks_received[i]` is `true` if block `i` has been received.
        blocks_received: BitVec<u8, Msb0>,
    },

    /// The piece has been fully downloaded and verified against its hash.
    ///
    /// A piece in this state has been:
    /// 1. Completely downloaded (all blocks received)
    /// 2. Hash-verified (computed SHA1 matches expected hash)
    /// 3. Written to disk
    ///
    /// Once verified, a piece does not need to be re-downloaded unless the
    /// on-disk data becomes corrupted (detected on read).
    Verified,

    /// The piece failed hash verification and needs to be re-downloaded.
    ///
    /// This state indicates that all blocks were received but the computed
    /// SHA1 hash did not match the expected hash from the metainfo. The piece
    /// data has been discarded and must be re-downloaded.
    ///
    /// # Retry Behavior
    ///
    /// A failed piece will transition to `InProgress` when retry begins.
    /// The download manager may choose to:
    /// - Request blocks from different peers than before
    /// - Blacklist peers that contributed to the failed piece
    /// - Implement retry limits to avoid infinite loops
    Failed,
}

impl PieceState {
    /// Returns `true` if the piece is in the `Missing` state.
    #[inline]
    pub fn is_missing(&self) -> bool {
        matches!(self, PieceState::Missing)
    }

    /// Returns `true` if the piece is in the `InProgress` state.
    #[inline]
    pub fn is_in_progress(&self) -> bool {
        matches!(self, PieceState::InProgress { .. })
    }

    /// Returns `true` if the piece is in the `Verified` state.
    #[inline]
    pub fn is_verified(&self) -> bool {
        matches!(self, PieceState::Verified)
    }

    /// Returns `true` if the piece is in the `Failed` state.
    #[inline]
    pub fn is_failed(&self) -> bool {
        matches!(self, PieceState::Failed)
    }

    /// Returns `true` if the piece needs to be downloaded (Missing or Failed).
    ///
    /// This is useful for piece selection: when choosing which piece to
    /// request next, we consider both pieces we've never started and pieces
    /// that failed verification.
    #[inline]
    pub fn needs_download(&self) -> bool {
        matches!(self, PieceState::Missing | PieceState::Failed)
    }

    /// Creates a new `InProgress` state with the given number of blocks.
    ///
    /// All blocks are initially marked as not received (bits set to false).
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `num_blocks` - The total number of blocks in this piece
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::PieceState;
    ///
    /// // A 256 KiB piece with 16 KiB blocks has 16 blocks
    /// let state = PieceState::new_in_progress(16);
    /// assert!(state.is_in_progress());
    /// ```
    pub fn new_in_progress(num_blocks: usize) -> Self {
        PieceState::InProgress {
            blocks_received: bitvec![u8, Msb0; 0; num_blocks],
        }
    }

    /// Marks a block as received. Returns `true` if this was a new block.
    ///
    /// If the piece is not in `InProgress` state, this method returns `false`
    /// without modifying the state. This handles the edge case where a late
    /// block arrives after the piece has already been verified or marked as
    /// failed.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `block_index` - The index of the block that was received
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `true` if the block was newly received (was not previously set)
    /// * `false` if the block was already received, or if the state is not
    ///   `InProgress`, or if `block_index` is out of bounds
    pub fn mark_block_received(&mut self, block_index: usize) -> bool {
        if let PieceState::InProgress { blocks_received } = self {
            if block_index < blocks_received.len() {
                let was_missing = !blocks_received[block_index];
                blocks_received.set(block_index, true);
                return was_missing;
            }
        }
        false
    }

    /// Returns the number of blocks received so far.
    ///
    /// Returns 0 if the piece is in `Missing` or `Failed` state.
    /// Returns the total block count if in `Verified` state (conceptually
    /// all blocks have been received and verified).
    ///
    /// # Note
    ///
    /// For `Verified` pieces, this method cannot return the actual count
    /// since we don't store the block count after verification. Callers
    /// should typically check `is_verified()` separately.
    pub fn blocks_received_count(&self) -> usize {
        match self {
            PieceState::InProgress { blocks_received } => {
                blocks_received.count_ones()
            }
            _ => 0,
        }
    }

    /// Returns the total number of blocks in this piece.
    ///
    /// Returns `None` if the piece is not in `InProgress` state.
    pub fn total_blocks(&self) -> Option<usize> {
        match self {
            PieceState::InProgress { blocks_received } => Some(blocks_received.len()),
            _ => None,
        }
    }

    /// Returns `true` if all blocks have been received.
    ///
    /// This is only meaningful for `InProgress` pieces. Returns `false` for
    /// all other states.
    pub fn all_blocks_received(&self) -> bool {
        match self {
            PieceState::InProgress { blocks_received } => blocks_received.all(),
            _ => false,
        }
    }

    /// Checks if a specific block has been received.
    ///
    /// Returns `false` if the piece is not in `InProgress` state or if the
    /// block index is out of bounds.
    pub fn has_block(&self, block_index: usize) -> bool {
        match self {
            PieceState::InProgress { blocks_received } => {
                block_index < blocks_received.len() && blocks_received[block_index]
            }
            _ => false,
        }
    }
}
