\subsection{Piece Hash Verification}

The fundamental integrity guarantee of BitTorrent is that every piece has a known
SHA1 hash stored in the torrent metadata. After downloading all blocks of a piece,
we must compute the SHA1 hash of the assembled data and compare it against the
expected hash. Only pieces that pass this verification should be written to disk.

The verification function is straightforward:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Accept the piece index (for error reporting), the raw piece data, and the
  expected 20-byte hash.
\item Compute the SHA1 hash of the data.
\item Compare the computed hash against the expected hash.
\item Return [[Ok(())]] on match, or [[Err(PieceHashMismatch)]] on mismatch.
\end{enumerate}

We include the piece index in the error because a typical download involves
thousands of pieces, and knowing which one failed is essential for debugging.
The error includes both the expected and computed hashes as hex strings, making
it easy to log and diagnose issues without additional formatting.

<<piece verification>>=
use sha1::{Sha1, Digest};

/// Verifies that a piece's data matches its expected SHA1 hash.
///
/// After downloading all blocks of a piece and assembling them into a contiguous
/// buffer, call this function to verify the data's integrity. BitTorrent stores
/// a 20-byte SHA1 hash for each piece in the torrent metadata; this function
/// computes the hash of the downloaded data and compares it.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `piece_index` - The index of the piece being verified (for error reporting)
/// * `data` - The complete piece data (all blocks assembled)
/// * `expected_hash` - The 20-byte SHA1 hash from the torrent metadata
///
/// # Returns
///
/// * `Ok(())` if the computed hash matches the expected hash
/// * `Err(PieceError::PieceHashMismatch)` if the hashes differ
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::{verify_piece, PieceError};
///
/// // "test" hashes to a94a8fe5ccb19ba61c4c0873d391e987982fbbd3
/// let expected: [u8; 20] = [
///     0xa9, 0x4a, 0x8f, 0xe5, 0xcc, 0xb1, 0x9b, 0xa6, 0x1c, 0x4c,
///     0x08, 0x73, 0xd3, 0x91, 0xe9, 0x87, 0x98, 0x2f, 0xbb, 0xd3,
/// ];
///
/// // Correct data should verify successfully
/// assert!(verify_piece(0, b"test", &expected).is_ok());
///
/// // Corrupted data should fail verification
/// assert!(verify_piece(0, b"Test", &expected).is_err());
/// ```
///
/// # Performance Notes
///
/// SHA1 hashing is CPU-bound. For typical piece sizes (256 KiB to 4 MiB),
/// verification takes microseconds to milliseconds. Consider running verification
/// in a background task if you need to avoid blocking the main download loop.
pub fn verify_piece(
    piece_index: u32,
    data: &[u8],
    expected_hash: &[u8; 20],
) -> Result<(), PieceError> {
    // Compute the SHA1 hash of the piece data
    let mut hasher = Sha1::new();
    hasher.update(data);
    let result = hasher.finalize();

    // Convert GenericArray to fixed-size array for comparison
    let mut computed_hash = [0u8; 20];
    computed_hash.copy_from_slice(&result);

    // Compare the hashes
    if computed_hash == *expected_hash {
        Ok(())
    } else {
        Err(PieceError::hash_mismatch(piece_index, expected_hash, &computed_hash))
    }
}
@

The design deliberately keeps the function simple and focused. We do not:
\begin{itemize}
\item Validate the piece index (the caller should do bounds checking separately)
\item Check the data length (different pieces can have different sizes)
\item Log anything (the error contains all needed debugging info)
\end{itemize}

This separation of concerns keeps the function testable and reusable across
different contexts (normal download, resume verification, seeding validation).

\subsection{Piece Manager}

The [[PieceManager]] is the central coordinator for piece download state. While
[[PieceState]] tracks the state of a single piece, the manager holds the state
for all pieces in the torrent and provides methods to query and transition
between states.

The manager stores four essential pieces of information:
\begin{description}
\item[piece\_states] A vector of [[PieceState]] for each piece in the torrent.
  This is the core state tracking structure.
\item[piece\_length] The standard piece length from the torrent metadata. Most
  pieces have this size, except possibly the last piece.
\item[total\_length] The total content length of the torrent. Combined with
  [[piece\_length]], this determines the size of the last piece.
\item[piece\_hashes] The 20-byte SHA1 hashes for each piece, extracted from the
  torrent metadata. Used for verification after downloading.
\end{description}

The manager deliberately does not handle networking or disk I/O---those are
separate concerns handled by other components. This makes the manager easy to
test and reason about: it is a pure state machine with well-defined transitions.

<<piece manager>>=
/// Central coordinator for piece download state.
///
/// PieceManager tracks the download state of all pieces in a torrent. It holds:
/// - The state of each piece (Missing, InProgress, Verified, Failed)
/// - Torrent geometry (piece_length, total_length, piece count)
/// - Expected piece hashes for verification
///
/// # Responsibilities
///
/// The manager's sole responsibility is state tracking. It does NOT:
/// - Handle network I/O (sending/receiving blocks)
/// - Write data to disk
/// - Manage peer connections
///
/// These concerns are handled by other components that use the manager to
/// query and update piece state.
///
/// # Thread Safety
///
/// This struct is not thread-safe. In a multi-threaded context, wrap it in
/// a `Mutex` or use message-passing to serialize access.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::PieceManager;
///
/// // Create a manager for a torrent with 3 pieces of 100 bytes each
/// // (total 300 bytes, so last piece is also 100 bytes)
/// let hashes: Vec<[u8; 20]> = vec![[0u8; 20]; 3];
/// let manager = PieceManager::new(100, 300, hashes).unwrap();
///
/// assert_eq!(manager.num_pieces(), 3);
/// assert!(manager.get_state(0).unwrap().is_missing());
/// ```
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct PieceManager {
    /// State of each piece, indexed by piece number.
    piece_states: Vec<PieceState>,

    /// Standard piece length from torrent metadata (bytes).
    piece_length: u32,

    /// Total content length of the torrent (bytes).
    total_length: u64,

    /// SHA1 hashes for each piece, for verification.
    piece_hashes: Vec<[u8; 20]>,
}

impl PieceManager {
    /// Creates a new PieceManager for a torrent.
    ///
    /// Validates that the number of hashes matches the computed piece count
    /// based on `piece_length` and `total_length`.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_length` - Standard piece length from torrent metadata
    /// * `total_length` - Total content length in bytes
    /// * `piece_hashes` - SHA1 hash for each piece (20 bytes each)
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `Ok(PieceManager)` if the hash count matches the computed piece count
    /// * `Err(PieceError::InvalidPieceIndex)` if the hash count is wrong
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::PieceManager;
    ///
    /// // 1000 bytes with 256-byte pieces = 4 pieces
    /// let hashes = vec![[0u8; 20]; 4];
    /// let manager = PieceManager::new(256, 1000, hashes);
    /// assert!(manager.is_ok());
    /// ```
    pub fn new(
        piece_length: u32,
        total_length: u64,
        piece_hashes: Vec<[u8; 20]>,
    ) -> Result<Self, PieceError> {
        // Compute expected piece count using ceiling division
        let expected_pieces = if total_length == 0 {
            0
        } else {
            ((total_length + piece_length as u64 - 1) / piece_length as u64) as usize
        };

        if piece_hashes.len() != expected_pieces {
            return Err(PieceError::InvalidPieceIndex {
                index: piece_hashes.len() as u32,
                num_pieces: expected_pieces as u32,
            });
        }

        // Initialize all pieces as Missing
        let piece_states = vec![PieceState::Missing; expected_pieces];

        Ok(PieceManager {
            piece_states,
            piece_length,
            total_length,
            piece_hashes,
        })
    }

    /// Returns the number of pieces in this torrent.
    #[inline]
    pub fn num_pieces(&self) -> u32 {
        self.piece_states.len() as u32
    }

    /// Returns the standard piece length.
    #[inline]
    pub fn piece_length(&self) -> u32 {
        self.piece_length
    }

    /// Returns the total content length.
    #[inline]
    pub fn total_length(&self) -> u64 {
        self.total_length
    }

    /// Returns a reference to the state of a specific piece.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The zero-based index of the piece
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `Ok(&PieceState)` if the index is valid
    /// * `Err(PieceError::InvalidPieceIndex)` if the index is out of range
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::PieceManager;
    ///
    /// let hashes = vec![[0u8; 20]; 3];
    /// let manager = PieceManager::new(100, 300, hashes).unwrap();
    ///
    /// assert!(manager.get_state(0).is_ok());
    /// assert!(manager.get_state(999).is_err());
    /// ```
    pub fn get_state(&self, piece_index: u32) -> Result<&PieceState, PieceError> {
        self.piece_states
            .get(piece_index as usize)
            .ok_or_else(|| PieceError::invalid_index(piece_index, self.num_pieces()))
    }

    /// Returns the expected SHA1 hash for a piece.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The zero-based index of the piece
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `Ok(&[u8; 20])` if the index is valid
    /// * `Err(PieceError::InvalidPieceIndex)` if the index is out of range
    pub fn get_hash(&self, piece_index: u32) -> Result<&[u8; 20], PieceError> {
        self.piece_hashes
            .get(piece_index as usize)
            .ok_or_else(|| PieceError::invalid_index(piece_index, self.num_pieces()))
    }

    /// Calculates the size of a specific piece.
    ///
    /// Most pieces have the standard `piece_length`, but the last piece may
    /// be smaller if `total_length` is not evenly divisible.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The zero-based index of the piece
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `Ok(u32)` with the piece size in bytes
    /// * `Err(PieceError::InvalidPieceIndex)` if the index is out of range
    pub fn get_piece_size(&self, piece_index: u32) -> Result<u32, PieceError> {
        if piece_index >= self.num_pieces() {
            return Err(PieceError::invalid_index(piece_index, self.num_pieces()));
        }
        Ok(piece_size(
            piece_index,
            self.piece_length,
            self.total_length,
            self.num_pieces(),
        ))
    }

    /// Starts downloading a piece, transitioning it to InProgress state.
    ///
    /// This method transitions a piece from Missing or Failed to InProgress.
    /// It initializes the block tracking BitVec based on the piece size.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The zero-based index of the piece to start
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `Ok(())` if the piece was successfully transitioned to InProgress
    /// * `Err(PieceError::InvalidPieceIndex)` if the index is out of range
    ///
    /// # State Transitions
    ///
    /// * `Missing` -> `InProgress`: Normal case when starting a new piece
    /// * `Failed` -> `InProgress`: Retry case after verification failure
    /// * `InProgress` -> `InProgress`: No-op (already downloading)
    /// * `Verified` -> Error: Cannot restart a verified piece
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::PieceManager;
    ///
    /// let hashes = vec![[0u8; 20]; 3];
    /// let mut manager = PieceManager::new(100, 300, hashes).unwrap();
    ///
    /// assert!(manager.get_state(0).unwrap().is_missing());
    /// manager.start_piece(0).unwrap();
    /// assert!(manager.get_state(0).unwrap().is_in_progress());
    /// ```
    pub fn start_piece(&mut self, piece_index: u32) -> Result<(), PieceError> {
        if piece_index >= self.num_pieces() {
            return Err(PieceError::invalid_index(piece_index, self.num_pieces()));
        }

        let idx = piece_index as usize;
        let current = &self.piece_states[idx];

        // Only transition from Missing or Failed
        if current.is_verified() {
            // Already verified, nothing to do (or could be an error)
            return Ok(());
        }

        if current.is_in_progress() {
            // Already in progress, nothing to do
            return Ok(());
        }

        // Calculate number of blocks for this piece
        let ps = self.get_piece_size(piece_index)?;
        let num_blocks = blocks_per_piece(ps) as usize;

        self.piece_states[idx] = PieceState::new_in_progress(num_blocks);
        Ok(())
    }

    /// Marks a block as received within a piece.
    ///
    /// This method should be called when block data is received from a peer.
    /// It updates the piece's block tracking BitVec.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The zero-based index of the piece
    /// * `block_index` - The zero-based index of the block within the piece
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `Ok(true)` if this was a new block (not previously received)
    /// * `Ok(false)` if the block was already received (duplicate)
    /// * `Err(PieceError::InvalidPieceIndex)` if the piece index is out of range
    ///
    /// # Note
    ///
    /// If the piece is not in InProgress state, returns `Ok(false)`.
    /// This handles late-arriving blocks after verification/failure gracefully.
    pub fn mark_block_received(
        &mut self,
        piece_index: u32,
        block_index: usize,
    ) -> Result<bool, PieceError> {
        if piece_index >= self.num_pieces() {
            return Err(PieceError::invalid_index(piece_index, self.num_pieces()));
        }

        let idx = piece_index as usize;
        let was_new = self.piece_states[idx].mark_block_received(block_index);
        Ok(was_new)
    }

    /// Checks if all blocks of a piece have been received.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The zero-based index of the piece
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `Ok(true)` if all blocks have been received
    /// * `Ok(false)` if blocks are still missing or piece is not InProgress
    /// * `Err(PieceError::InvalidPieceIndex)` if the index is out of range
    pub fn all_blocks_received(&self, piece_index: u32) -> Result<bool, PieceError> {
        let state = self.get_state(piece_index)?;
        Ok(state.all_blocks_received())
    }

    /// Marks a piece as verified (hash matched).
    ///
    /// Call this after successfully verifying the piece's SHA1 hash.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The zero-based index of the piece
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `Ok(())` if the piece was successfully marked as verified
    /// * `Err(PieceError::InvalidPieceIndex)` if the index is out of range
    pub fn mark_verified(&mut self, piece_index: u32) -> Result<(), PieceError> {
        if piece_index >= self.num_pieces() {
            return Err(PieceError::invalid_index(piece_index, self.num_pieces()));
        }

        self.piece_states[piece_index as usize] = PieceState::Verified;
        Ok(())
    }

    /// Marks a piece as failed (hash mismatch).
    ///
    /// Call this when piece verification fails. The piece can later be
    /// restarted with `start_piece()`.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The zero-based index of the piece
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `Ok(())` if the piece was successfully marked as failed
    /// * `Err(PieceError::InvalidPieceIndex)` if the index is out of range
    pub fn mark_failed(&mut self, piece_index: u32) -> Result<(), PieceError> {
        if piece_index >= self.num_pieces() {
            return Err(PieceError::invalid_index(piece_index, self.num_pieces()));
        }

        self.piece_states[piece_index as usize] = PieceState::Failed;
        Ok(())
    }

    /// Returns the number of verified pieces.
    pub fn verified_count(&self) -> usize {
        self.piece_states.iter().filter(|s| s.is_verified()).count()
    }

    /// Returns the number of pieces that need downloading (Missing or Failed).
    pub fn pending_count(&self) -> usize {
        self.piece_states.iter().filter(|s| s.needs_download()).count()
    }

    /// Returns true if all pieces are verified.
    pub fn is_complete(&self) -> bool {
        self.piece_states.iter().all(|s| s.is_verified())
    }

    /// Checks if a specific piece has been verified (hash matched).
    ///
    /// This is used to determine if we still need a piece when deciding
    /// whether to become interested in a peer.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The zero-based index of the piece
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `true` if the piece is verified
    /// * `false` if the piece is missing, in progress, failed, or index is invalid
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::PieceManager;
    ///
    /// let hashes = vec![[0u8; 20]; 3];
    /// let mut manager = PieceManager::new(100, 300, hashes).unwrap();
    ///
    /// assert!(!manager.is_verified(0)); // Not yet verified
    /// manager.mark_verified(0).unwrap();
    /// assert!(manager.is_verified(0));  // Now verified
    /// assert!(!manager.is_verified(100)); // Invalid index returns false
    /// ```
    pub fn is_verified(&self, piece_index: usize) -> bool {
        self.piece_states
            .get(piece_index)
            .map(|s| s.is_verified())
            .unwrap_or(false)
    }
}
