\subsection{In-Progress Piece Buffer}

When downloading a piece, we need to buffer the incoming blocks before the piece
is complete and can be verified. The [[InProgressPiece]] struct provides this
buffering capability, holding both the raw data and tracking which blocks have
been received.

The design separates concerns: [[PieceState]] tracks the abstract state of a piece
(Missing, InProgress, Verified, Failed), while [[InProgressPiece]] holds the actual
byte data for a piece currently being downloaded. This separation allows the piece
manager to remain lightweight---it does not hold megabytes of data, only state bits.

The [[InProgressPiece]] struct has two main components:

\begin{description}
\item[data] A [[Vec<u8>]] pre-allocated to the full piece size. Blocks are written
  directly into their correct positions within this buffer.
\item[blocks\_received] A [[BitVec]] tracking which blocks have been received. This
  mirrors the tracking in [[PieceState::InProgress]] and is used to detect completion.
\end{description}

When receiving a block, we perform several validations:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Block alignment}: The offset must be a multiple of [[BLOCK_SIZE]]
  (or zero). Non-aligned offsets indicate protocol violations or bugs.
\item \textbf{Bounds checking}: The block must fit entirely within the piece.
  The offset plus data length must not exceed the piece size.
\item \textbf{Duplicate detection}: If a block has already been received, we
  silently ignore the duplicate. This is not an error condition.
\end{enumerate}

<<in-progress piece>>=
/// Buffer for accumulating block data during piece download.
///
/// `InProgressPiece` holds the raw bytes of a piece being downloaded, along with
/// tracking information about which blocks have been received. Once all blocks
/// are received, the caller can extract the complete piece data for verification.
///
/// # Usage Pattern
///
/// 1. Create with piece geometry: `InProgressPiece::new(piece_index, piece_size)`
/// 2. As blocks arrive, call `receive_block(offset, data)`
/// 3. Check `is_complete()` after each block
/// 4. When complete, call `take_data()` to get the assembled piece for verification
///
/// # Memory Management
///
/// The data buffer is pre-allocated to the full piece size on construction.
/// This avoids reallocations during download and ensures predictable memory usage.
/// For a typical 256 KiB piece, this is a modest allocation.
///
/// # Thread Safety
///
/// This struct is not thread-safe. In a multi-threaded context, wrap it in a
/// `Mutex` or use message-passing to serialize access.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::{InProgressPiece, BLOCK_SIZE};
///
/// // Create buffer for a 40000-byte piece (3 blocks: 16384 + 16384 + 7232)
/// let mut piece = InProgressPiece::new(0, 40000);
///
/// // Receive blocks (can be out of order)
/// piece.receive_block(BLOCK_SIZE, &[0u8; 16384]).unwrap();      // Block 1
/// piece.receive_block(0, &[0u8; 16384]).unwrap();                // Block 0
/// piece.receive_block(2 * BLOCK_SIZE, &[0u8; 7232]).unwrap();   // Block 2
///
/// assert!(piece.is_complete());
/// let data = piece.take_data();
/// assert_eq!(data.len(), 40000);
/// ```
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct InProgressPiece {
    /// Index of this piece within the torrent.
    piece_index: u32,

    /// Size of this piece in bytes.
    piece_size: u32,

    /// Buffer holding the piece data. Pre-allocated to `piece_size`.
    data: Vec<u8>,

    /// Bit vector tracking which blocks have been received.
    /// `blocks_received[i]` is true if block `i` has been received.
    blocks_received: BitVec<u8, Msb0>,
}

impl InProgressPiece {
    /// Creates a new buffer for an in-progress piece.
    ///
    /// The data buffer is pre-allocated and zero-initialized to the full piece
    /// size. The `blocks_received` bit vector is initialized with all bits false.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The zero-based index of this piece
    /// * `piece_size` - The size of this piece in bytes
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::InProgressPiece;
    ///
    /// let piece = InProgressPiece::new(5, 262144); // 256 KiB piece
    /// assert_eq!(piece.piece_index(), 5);
    /// assert_eq!(piece.piece_size(), 262144);
    /// assert!(!piece.is_complete());
    /// ```
    pub fn new(piece_index: u32, piece_size: u32) -> Self {
        let num_blocks = blocks_per_piece(piece_size);
        InProgressPiece {
            piece_index,
            piece_size,
            data: vec![0u8; piece_size as usize],
            blocks_received: bitvec![u8, Msb0; 0; num_blocks as usize],
        }
    }

    /// Returns the piece index.
    #[inline]
    pub fn piece_index(&self) -> u32 {
        self.piece_index
    }

    /// Returns the piece size in bytes.
    #[inline]
    pub fn piece_size(&self) -> u32 {
        self.piece_size
    }

    /// Returns the number of blocks in this piece.
    #[inline]
    pub fn num_blocks(&self) -> u32 {
        self.blocks_received.len() as u32
    }

    /// Returns the number of blocks received so far.
    #[inline]
    pub fn blocks_received_count(&self) -> u32 {
        self.blocks_received.count_ones() as u32
    }

    /// Checks if a specific block has been received.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `block_index` - The zero-based index of the block
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// `true` if the block has been received, `false` otherwise.
    /// Returns `false` if `block_index` is out of bounds.
    pub fn has_block(&self, block_index: u32) -> bool {
        let idx = block_index as usize;
        idx < self.blocks_received.len() && self.blocks_received[idx]
    }

    /// Receives a block of data at the specified offset.
    ///
    /// The block data is copied into the buffer at the correct position, and
    /// the corresponding bit in `blocks_received` is set.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `offset` - Byte offset within the piece where this block starts.
    ///   Must be a multiple of `BLOCK_SIZE` (or 0).
    /// * `block_data` - The raw block data received from a peer.
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `Ok(true)` - Block was newly received and stored
    /// * `Ok(false)` - Block was a duplicate (already received); no-op
    /// * `Err(PieceError::InvalidBlockOffset)` - Offset not block-aligned or
    ///   block extends past piece end
    ///
    /// # Validation
    ///
    /// This method validates that:
    /// 1. The offset is a multiple of `BLOCK_SIZE`
    /// 2. The offset is within the piece bounds
    /// 3. The block (offset + data length) does not extend past the piece end
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::{InProgressPiece, BLOCK_SIZE};
    ///
    /// let mut piece = InProgressPiece::new(0, 40000);
    ///
    /// // Valid block at offset 0
    /// assert!(piece.receive_block(0, &[1u8; 16384]).unwrap());
    ///
    /// // Duplicate block is a no-op
    /// assert!(!piece.receive_block(0, &[2u8; 16384]).unwrap());
    ///
    /// // Invalid: non-aligned offset
    /// assert!(piece.receive_block(100, &[0u8; 100]).is_err());
    /// ```
    pub fn receive_block(&mut self, offset: u32, block_data: &[u8]) -> Result<bool, PieceError> {
        // Validate block alignment (offset must be multiple of BLOCK_SIZE)
        if offset != 0 && offset % BLOCK_SIZE != 0 {
            return Err(PieceError::InvalidBlockOffset {
                piece_index: self.piece_index,
                offset,
                piece_size: self.piece_size,
            });
        }

        // Validate offset is within piece bounds
        if offset >= self.piece_size {
            return Err(PieceError::InvalidBlockOffset {
                piece_index: self.piece_index,
                offset,
                piece_size: self.piece_size,
            });
        }

        // Validate block does not extend past piece end
        let end_offset = offset as u64 + block_data.len() as u64;
        if end_offset > self.piece_size as u64 {
            return Err(PieceError::InvalidBlockOffset {
                piece_index: self.piece_index,
                offset,
                piece_size: self.piece_size,
            });
        }

        // Calculate block index from offset
        let block_index = (offset / BLOCK_SIZE) as usize;

        // Check for duplicate
        if block_index < self.blocks_received.len() && self.blocks_received[block_index] {
            return Ok(false); // Duplicate, no-op
        }

        // Copy data into buffer
        let start = offset as usize;
        let end = start + block_data.len();
        self.data[start..end].copy_from_slice(block_data);

        // Mark block as received
        if block_index < self.blocks_received.len() {
            self.blocks_received.set(block_index, true);
        }

        Ok(true)
    }

    /// Returns `true` if all blocks have been received.
    ///
    /// When this returns `true`, the piece data is complete and ready for
    /// hash verification.
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::InProgressPiece;
    ///
    /// // Small piece that fits in one block
    /// let mut piece = InProgressPiece::new(0, 1000);
    /// assert!(!piece.is_complete());
    ///
    /// piece.receive_block(0, &[0u8; 1000]).unwrap();
    /// assert!(piece.is_complete());
    /// ```
    #[inline]
    pub fn is_complete(&self) -> bool {
        self.blocks_received.all()
    }

    /// Returns a reference to the piece data.
    ///
    /// The returned slice has length equal to `piece_size`. Blocks that have
    /// not been received will contain zeros (or stale data from previous
    /// failed attempts, if the buffer is reused).
    ///
    /// # Note
    ///
    /// Typically you should only call this when `is_complete()` returns `true`.
    /// Accessing incomplete data may return partial or zeroed content.
    pub fn data(&self) -> &[u8] {
        &self.data[..self.piece_size as usize]
    }

    /// Consumes the buffer and returns the piece data.
    ///
    /// This method takes ownership of the data buffer, avoiding a copy.
    /// After calling this, the `InProgressPiece` is consumed.
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::InProgressPiece;
    ///
    /// let mut piece = InProgressPiece::new(0, 100);
    /// piece.receive_block(0, &[42u8; 100]).unwrap();
    ///
    /// let data = piece.take_data();
    /// assert_eq!(data.len(), 100);
    /// assert_eq!(data[0], 42);
    /// ```
    pub fn take_data(mut self) -> Vec<u8> {
        self.data.truncate(self.piece_size as usize);
        self.data
    }

    /// Resets the buffer for reuse (e.g., after verification failure).
    ///
    /// This clears all received block markers and zeros the data buffer.
    /// The piece geometry (index, size) remains unchanged.
    ///
    /// # Use Case
    ///
    /// When a piece fails hash verification, we need to re-download it.
    /// Rather than allocating a new buffer, we can reset and reuse the
    /// existing one.
    pub fn reset(&mut self) {
        self.blocks_received.fill(false);
        self.data.fill(0);
    }
}
