\subsection{Rarest-First Piece Selection}

The rarest-first algorithm is the cornerstone of BitTorrent's piece selection
strategy. By preferentially downloading pieces that fewer peers have, we achieve
two critical goals:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Swarm health}: If a rare piece exists on only one peer and that
  peer disconnects before anyone else downloads it, the piece becomes unavailable
  to the entire swarm. By prioritizing rare pieces, we replicate them quickly,
  increasing their availability.
\item \textbf{Download speed}: Rare pieces are bottlenecks. Once we have a rare
  piece, we can upload it to other peers who want it, improving our reputation
  and reciprocation from the swarm.
\end{enumerate}

The [[select_rarest]] function combines information from three sources:

\begin{description}
\item[PieceManager] Tells us which pieces we already have (Verified) or are
  currently downloading (InProgress). We must exclude these from selection.
\item[PieceAvailability] Tells us how many peers have each piece across the
  entire swarm. Lower counts mean rarer pieces.
\item[Peer bitfield] Tells us which pieces a specific peer has. We can only
  request pieces that the peer actually has.
\end{description}

The algorithm proceeds as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Enumerate all piece indices
\item Filter out pieces we already have (Verified state)
\item Filter out pieces we're currently downloading (InProgress state)
\item Filter to only pieces the given peer has (bitfield check)
\item Sort remaining pieces by availability count (ascending---rarest first)
\item Return the sorted list of piece indices
\end{enumerate}

When multiple pieces have the same availability count, we use piece index as
a tiebreaker (lower indices first). This provides deterministic behavior for
testing and debugging.

<<rarest first selection>>=
/// Returns piece indices sorted by rarity (rarest first) that we need and a peer has.
///
/// This function implements the core rarest-first piece selection algorithm.
/// It combines piece state (what we have), availability (what's rare), and
/// peer bitfield (what this peer has) to produce a prioritized list of pieces
/// to request.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `manager` - The piece manager tracking our download state
/// * `availability` - Global availability counts across all peers
/// * `peer_bitfield` - The bitfield of pieces the peer has (MSB first per byte)
///
/// # Returns
///
/// A vector of piece indices sorted by availability (ascending). The first
/// element is the rarest piece that we need and the peer has. Returns an
/// empty vector if no suitable pieces exist.
///
/// # Filtering Rules
///
/// A piece is included in the result only if ALL of the following are true:
/// - The piece is NOT in Verified state (we don't have it yet)
/// - The piece is NOT in InProgress state (we're not already downloading it)
/// - The peer HAS the piece (bit is set in their bitfield)
/// - The piece has non-zero availability (at least one peer has it)
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::{PieceManager, PieceAvailability, select_rarest};
///
/// // Create a 4-piece torrent
/// let hashes = vec![[0u8; 20]; 4];
/// let mut manager = PieceManager::new(100, 400, hashes).unwrap();
/// let mut availability = PieceAvailability::new(4);
///
/// // Peer has all 4 pieces with varying availability
/// // Piece 0: 3 peers, Piece 1: 1 peer, Piece 2: 2 peers, Piece 3: 4 peers
/// availability.add_peer_bitfield(&[0b11110000]);
/// availability.add_peer_bitfield(&[0b10100000]);
/// availability.add_peer_bitfield(&[0b10100000]);
/// availability.add_peer_bitfield(&[0b10010000]);
///
/// // This peer has all pieces
/// let peer_bitfield = vec![0b11110000];
///
/// // Should return [1, 2, 0, 3] (sorted by availability: 1, 2, 3, 4)
/// let selection = select_rarest(&manager, &availability, &peer_bitfield);
/// assert_eq!(selection[0], 1); // Rarest piece (count=1)
/// ```
///
/// # Performance
///
/// Time complexity: O(n log n) where n is the number of pieces (due to sorting).
/// Space complexity: O(n) for the result vector.
///
/// For typical torrents with thousands of pieces, this is fast enough to call
/// frequently without concern.
pub fn select_rarest(
    manager: &PieceManager,
    availability: &PieceAvailability,
    peer_bitfield: &[u8],
) -> Vec<u32> {
    let num_pieces = manager.num_pieces();

    // Collect eligible pieces with their availability counts
    let mut candidates: Vec<(u32, u32)> = (0..num_pieces)
        .filter_map(|piece_index| {
            // Check if we need this piece (not Verified or InProgress)
            let state = manager.get_state(piece_index).ok()?;
            if state.is_verified() || state.is_in_progress() {
                return None;
            }

            // Check if peer has this piece
            let byte_index = piece_index as usize / 8;
            let bit_index = 7 - (piece_index as usize % 8);
            if byte_index >= peer_bitfield.len() {
                return None;
            }
            if (peer_bitfield[byte_index] >> bit_index) & 1 == 0 {
                return None;
            }

            // Get availability count (must be > 0 since peer has it)
            let count = availability.get_availability(piece_index)?;
            if count == 0 {
                // This shouldn't happen if peer_bitfield is consistent with
                // availability, but we handle it gracefully
                return None;
            }

            Some((piece_index, count))
        })
        .collect();

    // Sort by availability (ascending), then by piece index for stability
    candidates.sort_by(|a, b| {
        a.1.cmp(&b.1).then_with(|| a.0.cmp(&b.0))
    });

    // Extract just the piece indices
    candidates.into_iter().map(|(idx, _)| idx).collect()
}
