\subsection{Random Tiebreaker for Equally-Rare Pieces}

The rarest-first algorithm returns pieces sorted by availability count, but what
happens when multiple pieces have the same availability? The current implementation
uses piece index as a tiebreaker---lower indices come first. This deterministic
behavior is valuable for testing but problematic in production.

Consider a swarm where three pieces (indices 5, 12, and 27) all have availability
count 1. With deterministic tiebreaking, every leecher in the swarm will request
piece 5 first. This creates a ``herding'' effect:

\begin{itemize}
\item All leechers compete for the same piece from the same rare source
\item The peer holding piece 5 becomes overloaded with requests
\item Pieces 12 and 27 remain unreplicated even though they're equally rare
\item The swarm's overall replication is slower than it could be
\end{itemize}

The solution is to randomly select among pieces with equal availability. If we
need a piece and three candidates have the minimum availability count, we pick
one uniformly at random. This distributes load across rare piece holders and
accelerates replication of all rare pieces.

The [[select_piece_with_tiebreaker]] function takes an RNG as a parameter. This
dependency injection pattern enables:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Testability}: Tests can inject a seeded RNG for deterministic assertions
\item \textbf{Reproducibility}: Debug logging can record the seed to reproduce issues
\item \textbf{Flexibility}: Different RNG implementations (thread-local, crypto-secure)
\end{itemize}

<<random tiebreaker>>=
use rand::Rng;
use rand::seq::SliceRandom;

/// Selects a single piece from candidates with equal minimum availability.
///
/// This function implements random tiebreaking for rarest-first piece selection.
/// When multiple pieces have the same (lowest) availability count, we randomly
/// select among them rather than using a deterministic ordering like piece index.
///
/// # Why Random Selection Matters
///
/// Without randomization, all peers would request the same rare piece, creating
/// a bottleneck. By randomly selecting among equally-rare pieces, we distribute
/// load across rare piece holders and accelerate replication of all rare pieces.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `candidates` - Piece indices with their availability counts, as returned by
///   collecting pieces we need that a peer has
/// * `rng` - Random number generator for selection. Accepts any type implementing
///   [`rand::Rng`], enabling both seeded (for testing) and non-seeded (production)
///   usage.
///
/// # Returns
///
/// * `Some(piece_index)` - A randomly selected piece index from those with minimum
///   availability
/// * `None` - If the candidates slice is empty
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::select_piece_with_tiebreaker;
/// use rand::SeedableRng;
/// use rand::rngs::StdRng;
///
/// // Three pieces with availabilities: piece 5 has 1, pieces 12 and 27 have 1
/// let candidates = vec![(5u32, 1u32), (12, 1), (27, 1)];
///
/// // With a fixed seed, selection is deterministic
/// let mut rng = StdRng::seed_from_u64(42);
/// let selected = select_piece_with_tiebreaker(&candidates, &mut rng);
/// assert!(selected.is_some());
///
/// // The result is one of the minimum-availability pieces
/// let piece = selected.unwrap();
/// assert!([5, 12, 27].contains(&piece));
/// ```
///
/// # Algorithm
///
/// 1. Find the minimum availability count among all candidates
/// 2. Collect all piece indices with that minimum count
/// 3. Use the RNG to select uniformly at random from that subset
///
/// Time complexity: O(n) where n is the number of candidates.
/// Space complexity: O(k) where k is the number of pieces with minimum availability.
pub fn select_piece_with_tiebreaker<R: Rng>(
    candidates: &[(u32, u32)],
    rng: &mut R,
) -> Option<u32> {
    if candidates.is_empty() {
        return None;
    }

    // Find the minimum availability count
    let min_availability = candidates.iter().map(|(_, count)| *count).min()?;

    // Collect all pieces with the minimum availability
    let min_pieces: Vec<u32> = candidates
        .iter()
        .filter(|(_, count)| *count == min_availability)
        .map(|(idx, _)| *idx)
        .collect();

    // Randomly select one from the minimum-availability set
    min_pieces.choose(rng).copied()
}
@

\subsubsection{Random Tiebreaker Tests}

We test the tiebreaker with two complementary strategies:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Deterministic tests with fixed seed}: Using a seeded RNG, we verify
  that the function produces consistent results and correctly identifies the
  minimum-availability pieces.
\item \textbf{Statistical tests with random RNG}: Over many iterations, we verify
  that the selection distributes across all eligible pieces, not just one.
\end{enumerate}

<<random tiebreaker tests>>=
