\subsection{Request Scheduler}

The [[RequestScheduler]] is the central coordinator for block requests across all
peers. While the previous sections defined the building blocks---piece selection,
block selection, and request tracking---the scheduler combines them into a coherent
strategy.

The scheduler's primary responsibility is answering the question: ``Given the
current state of all peers and pieces, which blocks should I request from which
peers right now?'' This involves several interrelated decisions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Piece prioritization}: Using rarest-first selection, determine which
  pieces are most urgent to download.
\item \textbf{Peer matching}: For each desired piece, identify which unchoked peers
  have it.
\item \textbf{Request distribution}: Spread block requests across peers to maximize
  parallelism without exceeding per-peer pipeline limits.
\item \textbf{Duplicate avoidance}: In normal mode, avoid requesting the same block
  from multiple peers (endgame mode is the exception).
\item \textbf{Timeout detection}: Identify stale in-flight requests that should be
  cancelled and reassigned.
\end{enumerate}

The scheduler maintains a global view of all in-flight requests across all peers.
This is essential for duplicate avoidance: when deciding whether to request a block
from peer A, we need to know if we've already requested it from peer B. The PRD
specifies a 30-second timeout for requests, after which they can be reassigned.

\subsubsection{Configuration}

The scheduler accepts configuration via [[SchedulerConfig]], allowing tuning of
pipeline depth and timeout behavior.

<<scheduler config>>=
use std::time::Duration;

/// Configuration for the request scheduler.
///
/// These parameters control the scheduler's behavior regarding request
/// pipelining and timeout handling.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct SchedulerConfig {
    /// Maximum concurrent requests per peer.
    ///
    /// The BitTorrent protocol allows pipelining multiple requests to
    /// maximize throughput. The typical range is 10-16 requests per peer.
    /// Too few requests underutilizes the connection; too many can cause
    /// memory pressure and unfairness.
    ///
    /// Default: 10
    pub pipeline_limit: usize,

    /// Duration after which a request is considered timed out.
    ///
    /// Requests that exceed this duration without receiving data are
    /// marked as timed out and can be reassigned to other peers. The
    /// PRD specifies 30 seconds as the default.
    ///
    /// Default: 30 seconds
    pub request_timeout: Duration,
}

impl Default for SchedulerConfig {
    fn default() -> Self {
        SchedulerConfig {
            pipeline_limit: 10,
            request_timeout: Duration::from_secs(30),
        }
    }
}

impl SchedulerConfig {
    /// Creates a new configuration with custom pipeline limit.
    pub fn with_pipeline_limit(mut self, limit: usize) -> Self {
        self.pipeline_limit = limit;
        self
    }

    /// Creates a new configuration with custom timeout.
    pub fn with_timeout(mut self, timeout: Duration) -> Self {
        self.request_timeout = timeout;
        self
    }
}
@

\subsubsection{Pending Request Tracking}

The scheduler tracks all in-flight requests in a central data structure. This
enables:

\begin{itemize}
\item Checking whether a block is already requested (duplicate avoidance)
\item Finding requests that have timed out
\item Matching incoming piece messages to their corresponding requests
\item Counting requests per peer for pipeline limiting
\end{itemize}

We use a [[HashMap]] keyed by (piece\_index, offset) for O(1) lookup when checking
for duplicates or matching responses. A secondary structure tracks requests per
peer for efficient pipeline counting.

<<pending requests>>=
use std::collections::HashMap;

/// Key for identifying a specific block request.
///
/// A block is uniquely identified by its piece index and byte offset within
/// that piece. Length is not included because the protocol does not allow
/// requesting different lengths at the same offset.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
pub struct BlockKey {
    /// The piece containing this block.
    pub piece_index: u32,
    /// Byte offset within the piece.
    pub offset: u32,
}

impl BlockKey {
    /// Creates a new block key.
    pub fn new(piece_index: u32, offset: u32) -> Self {
        BlockKey { piece_index, offset }
    }
}

/// A tracked pending request with metadata.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct PendingRequest {
    /// The full request details.
    pub request: BlockRequest,
    /// Current state of the request.
    pub state: RequestState,
}

impl PendingRequest {
    /// Creates a new pending request in the InFlight state.
    pub fn new(request: BlockRequest) -> Self {
        PendingRequest {
            request,
            state: RequestState::InFlight,
        }
    }

    /// Returns the key for this request.
    pub fn key(&self) -> BlockKey {
        BlockKey::new(self.request.piece_index, self.request.offset)
    }

    /// Returns true if this request has timed out.
    pub fn is_timed_out(&self, timeout: Duration) -> bool {
        self.state == RequestState::InFlight && self.request.is_timed_out(timeout)
    }
}
@

\subsubsection{The Request Scheduler}

The [[RequestScheduler]] struct ties everything together. It maintains:

\begin{description}
\item[pending] A map from block keys to pending requests, enabling O(1) duplicate
  checking and response matching.
\item[peer\_request\_counts] A map from peer IDs to the count of in-flight requests
  to that peer, enabling O(1) pipeline limit checking.
\item[config] The scheduler configuration.
\end{description}

<<request scheduler>>=
/// Central coordinator for block requests across all peers.
///
/// The RequestScheduler manages which blocks to request from which peers,
/// implementing the request pipelining and timeout handling specified in
/// the PRD. It coordinates with the piece selection strategy to prioritize
/// rare pieces while respecting per-peer pipeline limits.
///
/// # Responsibilities
///
/// 1. **Pipeline management**: Track in-flight requests per peer, respecting
///    the configured pipeline limit (default 10, max 16).
/// 2. **Duplicate avoidance**: Ensure the same block is not requested from
///    multiple peers simultaneously (except in endgame mode).
/// 3. **Timeout detection**: Identify requests that have exceeded the timeout
///    threshold (default 30 seconds) for reassignment.
/// 4. **Request matching**: Match incoming piece messages to their requests.
///
/// # Usage Pattern
///
/// ```no_run
/// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::{
///     RequestScheduler, SchedulerConfig, PieceManager, PieceAvailability,
///     BlockRequest, BLOCK_SIZE,
/// };
///
/// // Create scheduler with default configuration
/// let mut scheduler = RequestScheduler::new(SchedulerConfig::default());
///
/// // When a peer becomes unchoked, generate requests
/// let peer_id = *b"-RS0001-xxxxxxxxxxxx";
/// let peer_bitfield = vec![0xFF]; // peer has all pieces
/// let hashes = vec![[0u8; 20]; 8];
/// let manager = PieceManager::new(BLOCK_SIZE * 4, BLOCK_SIZE as u64 * 4 * 8, hashes).unwrap();
/// let availability = PieceAvailability::new(8);
///
/// // The scheduler would be used with select_rarest and select_next_blocks
/// // to determine what to request
/// ```
///
/// # Thread Safety
///
/// This struct is not thread-safe. In a multi-threaded context, wrap it in
/// a `Mutex` or use message-passing to serialize access.
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct RequestScheduler {
    /// All pending requests, keyed by (piece_index, offset).
    pending: HashMap<BlockKey, PendingRequest>,

    /// Count of in-flight requests per peer.
    peer_request_counts: HashMap<PeerId, usize>,

    /// Scheduler configuration.
    config: SchedulerConfig,
}

impl RequestScheduler {
    /// Creates a new request scheduler with the given configuration.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `config` - The scheduler configuration
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::{RequestScheduler, SchedulerConfig};
    ///
    /// let scheduler = RequestScheduler::new(SchedulerConfig::default());
    /// assert_eq!(scheduler.pending_count(), 0);
    /// ```
    pub fn new(config: SchedulerConfig) -> Self {
        RequestScheduler {
            pending: HashMap::new(),
            peer_request_counts: HashMap::new(),
            config,
        }
    }

    /// Returns the scheduler configuration.
    pub fn config(&self) -> &SchedulerConfig {
        &self.config
    }

    /// Returns the total number of pending requests across all peers.
    pub fn pending_count(&self) -> usize {
        self.pending.len()
    }

    /// Returns the number of in-flight requests to a specific peer.
    pub fn peer_request_count(&self, peer: &PeerId) -> usize {
        self.peer_request_counts.get(peer).copied().unwrap_or(0)
    }

    /// Returns how many more requests can be sent to a peer.
    ///
    /// This is the difference between the pipeline limit and the current
    /// in-flight count for this peer.
    pub fn available_slots(&self, peer: &PeerId) -> usize {
        let current = self.peer_request_count(peer);
        self.config.pipeline_limit.saturating_sub(current)
    }

    /// Checks if a block is already pending (requested from any peer).
    ///
    /// This is used for duplicate avoidance: in normal mode, we should not
    /// request the same block from multiple peers simultaneously.
    pub fn is_block_pending(&self, piece_index: u32, offset: u32) -> bool {
        let key = BlockKey::new(piece_index, offset);
        self.pending.get(&key).map_or(false, |p| p.state == RequestState::InFlight)
    }

    /// Registers a new block request.
    ///
    /// The request is added to the pending set and the peer's request count
    /// is incremented. The caller should have already checked that:
    /// 1. The block is not already pending
    /// 2. The peer has not exceeded their pipeline limit
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `request` - The block request to register
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// `true` if the request was registered, `false` if a request for this
    /// block already exists.
    pub fn register_request(&mut self, request: BlockRequest) -> bool {
        let key = BlockKey::new(request.piece_index, request.offset);

        // Check for duplicate
        if self.pending.contains_key(&key) {
            return false;
        }

        let peer = request.peer;
        self.pending.insert(key, PendingRequest::new(request));
        *self.peer_request_counts.entry(peer).or_insert(0) += 1;
        true
    }

    /// Marks a block request as completed (data received).
    ///
    /// The request is removed from tracking and the peer's request count
    /// is decremented.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The piece index of the completed block
    /// * `offset` - The byte offset of the completed block
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// The completed request if it was found, `None` otherwise.
    pub fn complete_request(&mut self, piece_index: u32, offset: u32) -> Option<PendingRequest> {
        let key = BlockKey::new(piece_index, offset);

        if let Some(mut pending) = self.pending.remove(&key) {
            // Decrement peer count
            if let Some(count) = self.peer_request_counts.get_mut(&pending.request.peer) {
                *count = count.saturating_sub(1);
                if *count == 0 {
                    self.peer_request_counts.remove(&pending.request.peer);
                }
            }

            pending.state = RequestState::Completed;
            Some(pending)
        } else {
            None
        }
    }

    /// Cancels a block request.
    ///
    /// The request is removed from tracking and the peer's request count
    /// is decremented. This is used when:
    /// - A block is received from a different peer
    /// - A peer disconnects
    /// - The download is aborted
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The piece index of the block to cancel
    /// * `offset` - The byte offset of the block to cancel
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// The cancelled request if it was found, `None` otherwise.
    pub fn cancel_request(&mut self, piece_index: u32, offset: u32) -> Option<PendingRequest> {
        let key = BlockKey::new(piece_index, offset);

        if let Some(mut pending) = self.pending.remove(&key) {
            // Decrement peer count
            if let Some(count) = self.peer_request_counts.get_mut(&pending.request.peer) {
                *count = count.saturating_sub(1);
                if *count == 0 {
                    self.peer_request_counts.remove(&pending.request.peer);
                }
            }

            pending.state = RequestState::Cancelled;
            Some(pending)
        } else {
            None
        }
    }

    /// Cancels all requests to a specific peer.
    ///
    /// This is called when a peer disconnects or becomes choked. All
    /// requests to this peer are removed and returned so they can be
    /// reassigned to other peers.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `peer` - The peer whose requests should be cancelled
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// A vector of the cancelled requests.
    pub fn cancel_peer_requests(&mut self, peer: &PeerId) -> Vec<PendingRequest> {
        let mut cancelled = Vec::new();

        // Collect keys to remove
        let keys_to_remove: Vec<BlockKey> = self.pending
            .iter()
            .filter(|(_, p)| p.request.peer == *peer)
            .map(|(k, _)| *k)
            .collect();

        // Remove and collect
        for key in keys_to_remove {
            if let Some(mut pending) = self.pending.remove(&key) {
                pending.state = RequestState::Cancelled;
                cancelled.push(pending);
            }
        }

        // Clear peer count
        self.peer_request_counts.remove(peer);

        cancelled
    }

    /// Finds all timed-out requests and marks them for reassignment.
    ///
    /// This scans all pending requests and returns those that have exceeded
    /// the configured timeout. The requests are removed from tracking so they
    /// can be re-registered for different peers.
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// A vector of timed-out requests with their state set to `TimedOut`.
    pub fn collect_timed_out(&mut self) -> Vec<PendingRequest> {
        let timeout = self.config.request_timeout;
        let mut timed_out = Vec::new();

        // Collect keys of timed-out requests
        let keys_to_remove: Vec<BlockKey> = self.pending
            .iter()
            .filter(|(_, p)| p.is_timed_out(timeout))
            .map(|(k, _)| *k)
            .collect();

        // Remove and collect
        for key in keys_to_remove {
            if let Some(mut pending) = self.pending.remove(&key) {
                // Decrement peer count
                if let Some(count) = self.peer_request_counts.get_mut(&pending.request.peer) {
                    *count = count.saturating_sub(1);
                    if *count == 0 {
                        self.peer_request_counts.remove(&pending.request.peer);
                    }
                }

                pending.state = RequestState::TimedOut;
                timed_out.push(pending);
            }
        }

        timed_out
    }

    /// Returns the set of block offsets currently in-flight for a piece.
    ///
    /// This is useful when calling `select_next_blocks` to avoid requesting
    /// blocks that are already pending.
    pub fn in_flight_offsets_for_piece(&self, piece_index: u32) -> HashSet<u32> {
        self.pending
            .iter()
            .filter(|(k, p)| k.piece_index == piece_index && p.state == RequestState::InFlight)
            .map(|(k, _)| k.offset)
            .collect()
    }

    /// Generates block requests for a peer based on current state.
    ///
    /// This method combines rarest-first piece selection with block selection
    /// to determine which blocks should be requested from a peer. It respects
    /// the pipeline limit and avoids requesting blocks already pending.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `peer` - The peer to generate requests for
    /// * `peer_bitfield` - The peer's bitfield (which pieces they have)
    /// * `manager` - The piece manager tracking download state
    /// * `availability` - Global piece availability across the swarm
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// A vector of (piece_index, offset, length) tuples representing blocks
    /// to request. The caller should create `BlockRequest` objects and
    /// register them with `register_request`.
    pub fn request_blocks(
        &self,
        peer: &PeerId,
        peer_bitfield: &[u8],
        manager: &PieceManager,
        availability: &PieceAvailability,
    ) -> Vec<(u32, u32, u32)> {
        let available_slots = self.available_slots(peer);
        if available_slots == 0 {
            return Vec::new();
        }

        let mut requests = Vec::new();

        // Get pieces sorted by rarity that this peer has and we need
        let candidate_pieces = select_rarest(manager, availability, peer_bitfield);

        for piece_index in candidate_pieces {
            if requests.len() >= available_slots {
                break;
            }

            // Get in-flight offsets for this piece (from any peer)
            let in_flight = self.in_flight_offsets_for_piece(piece_index);

            // Select blocks we can request
            let remaining_slots = available_slots - requests.len();
            let blocks = select_next_blocks(manager, piece_index, &in_flight, remaining_slots);

            for (offset, length) in blocks {
                // Double-check not already pending (defensive)
                if !self.is_block_pending(piece_index, offset) {
                    requests.push((piece_index, offset, length));
                    if requests.len() >= available_slots {
                        break;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        requests
    }
}
