\subsection{Block Request Tracking}

While [[PieceState]] tracks which blocks have been received for a piece, we also
need to track outstanding block requests---the requests we have sent to peers
but not yet received responses for. This is essential for:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Timeout handling}: If a peer fails to respond within a reasonable
  time, we need to re-request the block from another peer.
\item \textbf{Request pipelining}: To maximize throughput, we send multiple
  requests before waiting for responses. We need to track which requests are
  in flight.
\item \textbf{Cancel coordination}: If we receive a block from one peer, we may
  want to cancel duplicate requests to other peers.
\item \textbf{Peer accounting}: Tracking which peer was asked for which block
  helps identify slow or unresponsive peers.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Peer Identity}

Before defining block requests, we need a way to identify peers. In BitTorrent,
each peer has a 20-byte identifier exchanged during the handshake. We define
a simple type alias for this:

<<peer id type>>=
use std::time::Instant;

/// A 20-byte peer identifier.
///
/// In the BitTorrent protocol, each client generates a unique peer ID that
/// identifies it across the swarm. This ID is:
/// - Sent to the tracker in announce requests
/// - Exchanged in the handshake with other peers
/// - Used to track which peer sent which blocks
///
/// The format typically encodes client name and version (e.g., "-TR2940-" for
/// Transmission 2.94), followed by random bytes.
pub type PeerId = [u8; 20];
@

\subsubsection{Block Request Structure}

A [[BlockRequest]] represents a single outstanding request for a block. It
captures everything needed to manage the request lifecycle:

<<block request>>=
/// A request for a single block of data from a peer.
///
/// BlockRequest tracks an outstanding request sent to a peer. The BitTorrent
/// protocol allows pipelining multiple requests (typically 10-16) to maximize
/// throughput. Each request must be tracked so we can:
///
/// - Detect timeouts and re-request from another peer
/// - Match incoming piece messages to their requests
/// - Cancel duplicate requests when a block is received
/// - Account for peer performance
///
/// # Fields
///
/// The request is fully specified by piece_index, offset, and length---these
/// match the parameters of the `request` message in the wire protocol. The
/// `requested_at` timestamp enables timeout detection, and `peer` identifies
/// who we asked.
///
/// # Typical Values
///
/// - `length`: Usually 16384 bytes (16 KiB, the standard block size)
/// - Pipeline depth: 10-16 concurrent requests per peer
/// - Timeout: 30 seconds (configurable)
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct BlockRequest {
    /// Index of the piece containing this block.
    ///
    /// Valid range is [0, num_pieces) where num_pieces comes from the
    /// torrent metadata.
    pub piece_index: u32,

    /// Byte offset within the piece where this block starts.
    ///
    /// Must be a multiple of the block size (16384) except possibly for
    /// the last block of the last piece.
    pub offset: u32,

    /// Length of the block in bytes.
    ///
    /// Standard block size is 16384 bytes. The last block of a piece may
    /// be smaller if the piece size is not evenly divisible by 16384.
    pub length: u32,

    /// When this request was sent.
    ///
    /// Used to detect timeouts. If `Instant::now() - requested_at` exceeds
    /// the timeout threshold, the request should be cancelled and re-sent
    /// to a different peer.
    pub requested_at: Instant,

    /// The peer this request was sent to.
    ///
    /// Used for:
    /// - Matching incoming piece messages to requests
    /// - Performance tracking (slow peers may be deprioritized)
    /// - Cancellation (send cancel message to this peer if block received
    ///   from another)
    pub peer: PeerId,
}

impl BlockRequest {
    /// Creates a new block request.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The piece containing this block
    /// * `offset` - Byte offset within the piece
    /// * `length` - Block length in bytes
    /// * `peer` - The peer this request is sent to
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use std::time::Instant;
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::BlockRequest;
    ///
    /// let peer_id = *b"-RS0001-xxxxxxxxxxxx";
    /// let request = BlockRequest::new(0, 0, 16384, peer_id);
    /// assert_eq!(request.piece_index, 0);
    /// assert_eq!(request.length, 16384);
    /// ```
    pub fn new(piece_index: u32, offset: u32, length: u32, peer: PeerId) -> Self {
        BlockRequest {
            piece_index,
            offset,
            length,
            requested_at: Instant::now(),
            peer,
        }
    }

    /// Returns true if this request has exceeded the given timeout duration.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `timeout` - Maximum duration to wait for a response
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use std::time::Duration;
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::BlockRequest;
    ///
    /// let peer_id = *b"-RS0001-xxxxxxxxxxxx";
    /// let request = BlockRequest::new(0, 0, 16384, peer_id);
    ///
    /// // Fresh request should not be timed out
    /// assert!(!request.is_timed_out(Duration::from_secs(30)));
    /// ```
    pub fn is_timed_out(&self, timeout: std::time::Duration) -> bool {
        self.requested_at.elapsed() > timeout
    }

    /// Returns the age of this request (time since it was sent).
    pub fn age(&self) -> std::time::Duration {
        self.requested_at.elapsed()
    }

    /// Returns true if this request matches the given piece and offset.
    ///
    /// This is used to match incoming `piece` messages to outstanding
    /// requests. The length is not checked because the response may have
    /// a different length (though this would be a protocol violation).
    pub fn matches(&self, piece_index: u32, offset: u32) -> bool {
        self.piece_index == piece_index && self.offset == offset
    }
}
@

\subsubsection{Request State Machine}

Each block request progresses through a lifecycle. Tracking this state enables
proper timeout handling, retries, and cancellation:

<<request state>>=
/// The lifecycle state of a block request.
///
/// A request transitions through these states as it progresses from being
/// sent to being fulfilled or failing. This state machine enables:
///
/// - **Queue management**: Only `Pending` requests are sent to peers
/// - **Timeout detection**: `InFlight` requests are monitored for timeouts
/// - **Completion tracking**: `Completed` requests can be removed from tracking
/// - **Retry logic**: `TimedOut` requests are re-queued for different peers
/// - **Cancellation**: `Cancelled` requests may need cancel messages sent
///
/// # State Transitions
///
/// ```text
/// Pending ──────> InFlight ──────> Completed
///                    │
///                    ├──────────> TimedOut ──────> (back to Pending)
///                    │
///                    └──────────> Cancelled
/// ```
///
/// # Design Notes
///
/// We use an enum rather than boolean flags because the states are mutually
/// exclusive and the transitions are well-defined. This makes invalid states
/// unrepresentable.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum RequestState {
    /// Request is queued but not yet sent to a peer.
    ///
    /// Pending requests are waiting for capacity in the pipeline (we limit
    /// concurrent requests per peer) or for a suitable peer to become
    /// available.
    Pending,

    /// Request has been sent to a peer and we are awaiting response.
    ///
    /// InFlight requests are actively being monitored for timeout. If the
    /// peer does not respond within the timeout period, the request
    /// transitions to TimedOut.
    InFlight,

    /// The block was successfully received and verified.
    ///
    /// Completed requests can be removed from tracking. The block data has
    /// been written to the piece buffer.
    Completed,

    /// The request timed out before receiving a response.
    ///
    /// Timed out requests should be re-sent to a different peer. The
    /// download manager may also send a `cancel` message to the original
    /// peer to avoid receiving duplicate data later.
    TimedOut,

    /// The request was cancelled before receiving a response.
    ///
    /// Cancellation happens when:
    /// - The block was received from a different peer (duplicate request)
    /// - The download was aborted
    /// - The peer disconnected
    Cancelled,
}

impl RequestState {
    /// Returns true if the request is in a terminal state.
    ///
    /// Terminal states are Completed and Cancelled. Requests in these
    /// states should be removed from active tracking.
    pub fn is_terminal(&self) -> bool {
        matches!(self, RequestState::Completed | RequestState::Cancelled)
    }

    /// Returns true if the request is still active (needs monitoring).
    ///
    /// Active states are Pending and InFlight. Requests in these states
    /// need to be tracked for timeout or sending.
    pub fn is_active(&self) -> bool {
        matches!(self, RequestState::Pending | RequestState::InFlight)
    }

    /// Returns true if the request can be retried.
    ///
    /// Only TimedOut requests can be retried. Pending and InFlight requests
    /// are still in progress, and Completed/Cancelled requests should not
    /// be retried.
    pub fn can_retry(&self) -> bool {
        matches!(self, RequestState::TimedOut)
    }
}
@

